# 13. QUALITY REQUIREMENTS AND ACCEPTANCE TESTS

The product will not be accepted as complete merely because its interface looks correct.

It must pass the following tests.

### Test 1 — Basic gate

- Insert a 7400.
- Connect switches to its input pins.
- Connect its output to an indicator.
- Test all four input combinations.
- Verify the NAND truth table.

### Test 2 — XOR

- Insert 7486.
- Connect its inputs and outputs.
- Verify all input combinations.

### Test 3 — Flip-flop

- Insert 7476 or 7474.
- Connect clock, control and output pins.
- Test the correct triggering edge.
- Test preset and clear behavior.

### Test 4 — Counter

- Insert 7490.
- Connect its required pins.
- Trigger the clock.
- Verify its actual count behavior and reset operation.

### Test 5 — Display

- Insert 7447.
- Connect appropriate BCD inputs.
- Connect the segment outputs to the onboard display.
- Verify displayed digits and control behavior.

### Test 6 — Memory

- Insert 6116 in a compatible DIP-24 location.
- Write data to different addresses.
- Read it back.
- Test chip selection and write enable.

### Test 7 — Patch cords

- Connect two actual terminals.
- Confirm the net is electrically connected.
- Move the cable.
- Confirm that the connection is retained.
- Remove the cable.
- Confirm that the electrical connection is removed.

### Test 8 — Project storage

- Build a circuit.
- Save it.
- Close the project.
- Reopen it.
- Verify all ICs, connections and supported states are preserved.

### Test 9 — Platform consistency

- Open the same project in the web application and Windows application.
- Confirm that the circuit structure and IC behavior are identical.

### Test 10 — Engineering accuracy

Test every IC against its datasheet-derived truth tables, control conditions, pin mapping and relevant clock/reset behavior.

